{"step_index":89,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:39:07Z","thinking":"**Defining Roman Dominating Functions**\n\nFuruya's paper is a short two pages, worth examining directly. A Roman dominating function maps vertices to {0, 1, 2}. The function defines sets: V0 (value 0), V1 (value 1), V2 (value 2). The weight is calculated as |V1| + 2|V2|. This function is Roman dominating if every vertex in V0 has a neighbor in V2. Essentially, V2 must dominate V0.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nimport random\n\n# For any graph G with no isolates, let f be a min-weight Roman dominating function.\n# How does one produce a total dominating set S with |S| <= weight(f)?\n# Let's test a greedy or constructive approach on thousands of graphs!\n\ndef find_min_rdf(G):\n    # return f with min weight\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    n = len(nodes)\n    import itertools\n    min_w = 2*n + 1\n    best_f = None\n    for f in itertools.product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\n        w = sum(f)\n        if w >= min_w:\n            continue\n        mapping = dict(zip(nodes, f))\n        if all(mapping[v] > 0 or any(mapping[u] == 2 for u in G.neighbors(v)) for v in nodes):\n            min_w = w\n            best_f = mapping\n    return best_f, min_w\n\n# Let's see: given f, how to choose S?\n# Notice: V2 dominates V0.\n# What vertices are NOT dominated by V2?\n# Only vertices in V1 cup V2 that have NO neighbor in V2.\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_40a99333","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Investigating RDF and total domination relation","toolSummary":"Investigate RDF and total dom"}}]}
