{"step_index":125,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:23Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport urllib.request, gzip\n\nurl = 'https://arxiv.org/e-print/1906.11743'\nreq = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0'})\nwith urllib.request.urlopen(req) as resp:\n    data = resp.read()\nlines = gzip.decompress(data).decode('utf-8', errors='ignore').splitlines()\nfor i, l in enumerate(lines):\n    if 'begin{prop}[Furuya' in l:\n        for j in range(max(0, i-5), min(len(lines), i+30)):\n            print(lines[j])\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_40a99333","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Viewing proposition by Furuya in 1906.11743","toolSummary":"View Furuya proposition"}}]}
{"step_index":126,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:25Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:40:25+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:40:25+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\nFor a function $f:V(G)\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{+}\\cup \\{0,\\infty \\}$, the {\\it weight} $w(f)$ of $f$ is defined by $w(f)=\\sum _{u\\in V(G)}f(u)$.\r\nLet $c\\in \\mathbb{R}^{+}\\cup \\{\\infty \\}$.\r\nA function $f:V(G)\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{+}\\cup \\{0,\\infty \\}$ is a {\\it $c$-self dominating function} (or {\\it $c$-SDF}) of $G$ if for each $u\\in V(G)$, $f(u)\\geq c$ or $\\max\\{f(v):v\\in N_{G}(u)\\}\\geq 1$.\r\nThen the following proposition holds.\r\n\r\n\\begin{prop}[Furuya~\\cite{F1}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\\label{prop1.1}\r\nLet $c\\in \\mathbb{R}^{+}\\cup \\{\\infty \\}$, and let $G$ be a graph.\r\nIf $f$ is a $c$-SDF of $G$, then there exists a $c$-SDF $g$ of $G$ such that $w(g)\\leq w(f)$ and $g(u)\\in \\{0,1,c\\}$ for all $u\\in V(G)$.\r\n\\end{prop}\r\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\r\nIt follows from Proposition~\\ref{prop1.1} that the minimum weight of a $c$-SDF of $G$ is well-defined.\r\nThe minimum weight of a $c$-SDF of $G$, denoted by $\\gamma ^{c}(G)$, is called the {\\it $c$-self domination number} of $G$.\r\n%A $c$-SDF $f$ of $G$ with $w(f)=\\gamma ^{c}(G)$ is called a {\\it $\\gamma ^{c}$-function} of $G$.\r\nNote that $\\gamma ^{1}(G)=\\gamma (G)$ and $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)=\\gamma _{t}(G)$ for all graphs $G$ (see \\cite{F1}).\r\nFurthermore, the $\\frac{1}{2}$-self domination number is equal to the half of the Roman domination number defined in Subsection~\\ref{sec1.1}.\r\nThus self domination concept is a common generalization of three well-studied invariants.\r\n\r\nIn this paper, our main aim is to analyze a behavior of the $c$-self domination number in Erd\\H{o}s-R\\'{e}nyi model random graphs $G(n,p)$ on $[n]:=\\{1,2,\\ldots ,n\\}$.\r\nFor $p\\in (0,1)$ and $n\\in \\mathbb{Z}^{+}\\setminus \\{1\\}$, let $a_{p}(n)=\\log _{1/(1-p)}\\frac{n}{\\log _{1/(1-p)}n\\ln n}$.\r\nThen the following are known.\r\n\r\n\\begin{Thm}[Wieland and Godbole~\\cite{WG}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\\label{ThmA}\r\nFor $p\\in (0,1)$, $\\gamma (G(n,p))\\in \\{\\lfloor a_{p}(n) \\rfloor +1,\\lfloor a_{p}(n) \\rfloor +2\\}$ with probability that tend to $1$ as $n\\rightarrow \\infty $.\r\n\\end{Thm}\r\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\r\n\\begin{Thm}[Bonato and Wang~\\cite{BW}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\\label{ThmB}\r\nFor $p\\in (0,1)$, $\\gamma _{t}(G(n,p))\\in \\{\\lfloor a_{p}(n) \\rfloor +1,\\lfloor a_{p}(n) \\rfloor +2\\}$ with probability that tend to $1$ as $n\\rightarrow \\infty $.\r\n\\end{Thm}\r\n%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n\r\n\n"}
