{"step_index":132,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:33Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:40:33+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:40:33+02:00\n\nThe command exited with code 0.\nOutput:\n200: Thus $\\{f(u):u\\in V(G)\\}\\subseteq \\{0,1,c\\}$.\r\n201: In particular, the minimum weight of $c$-SDF of $G$ is well-defined.\r\n202: \\end{remark}\r\n203: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n204: \r\n205: The minimum weight of a $c$-SDF of $G$, denoted by $\\gamma ^{c}(G)$, is called the {\\it $c$-self-domination number} of $G$.\r\n206: A $c$-SDF $f$ of $G$ with $w(f)=\\gamma ^{c}(G)$ is called a {\\it $\\gamma ^{c}$-function} of $G$.\r\n207: Considering Remark~\\ref{remark1},\r\n208: \\begin{align}\r\n209: \\mbox{$\\{f(u):u\\in V(G)\\}\\subseteq \\{0,1,c\\}$ for a $\\gamma ^{c}$-function $f$ of a graph $G$.}\\label{01c}\r\n210: \\end{align}\r\n211: \r\n212: We show that $c$-self-domination is a common generalization of domination, total domination and Roman domination.\r\n213: \r\n214: \\begin{prop}%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n215: \\label{prop1.1}\r\n216: Let $G$ be a graph.\r\n217: Then the following hold.\r\n218: \\begin{enumerate}\r\n219: \\item[{\\upshape(i)}]\r\n220: $\\gamma ^{1}(G)=\\gamma (G)$,\r\n221: \\item[{\\upshape(ii)}]\r\n222: $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)=\\gamma _{t}(G)$, and\r\n223: \\item[{\\upshape(iii)}]\r\n224: $\\gamma ^{\\frac{1}{2}}(G)=\\frac{1}{2}\\gamma _{R}(G)$.\r\n225: \\end{enumerate}\r\n226: \\end{prop}\r\n227: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n228: \\proof\r\n229: \\begin{enumerate}\r\n230: \\item[{\\upshape(i)}]\r\n231: For a dominating set $S$ of $G$ with $|S|=\\gamma (G)$, the function $f_{1}:V(G)\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{+}$ with\r\n232: $$\r\n233: f_{1}(u)=\r\n234: \\begin{cases}\r\n235: 1, & u\\in S;\\\\\r\n236: 0, & u\\notin S\r\n237: \\end{cases}\r\n238: $$\r\n239: is a $1$-SDF of $G$ with $w(f_{1})=|S|$, and hence $\\gamma ^{1}(G)\\leq w(f_{1})=|S|=\\gamma (G)$.\r\n240: \r\n241: Let $f$ be a $\\gamma ^{1}$-function of $G$.\r\n242: Then by (\\ref{01c}), $\\{f(u):u\\in V(G)\\}\\subseteq \\{0,1\\}$.\r\n243: Hence the set $S_{1}:=\\{u\\in V(G):f(u)=1\\}$ is a dominating set of $G$ with $|S_{1}|=w(f)$.\r\n244: Thus $\\gamma (G)\\leq |S_{1}|=w(f)=\\gamma ^{1}(G)$.\r\n245: \r\n246: Consequently, $\\gamma ^{1}(G)=\\gamma (G)$.\r\n247: \r\n248: \\item[{\\upshape(ii)}]\r\n249: If $G$ has an isolated vertex, then it is clear that $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)=\\infty =\\gamma _{t}(G)$.\r\n250: Thus we may assume that $G$ has no isolated vertices.\r\n251: For a total dominating set $S$ of $G$ with $|S|=\\gamma _{t}(G)$, the function $f_{2}:V(G)\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{+}$ with\r\n252: $$\r\n253: f_{2}(u)=\r\n254: \\begin{cases}\r\n255: 1, & u\\in S;\\\\\r\n256: 0, & u\\notin S\r\n257: \\end{cases}\r\n258: $$\r\n259: is an $\\infty $-SDF of $G$ with $w(f_{2})=|S|$, and hence $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)\\leq w(f_{2})=|S|=\\gamma _{t}(G)$.\r\n260: \r\n261: Let $f$ be a $\\gamma ^{\\infty }$-function of $G$.\r\n262: Since the function assigning $1$ to all vertices of $G$ is an $\\infty $-SDF of $G$, we have $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)<\\infty $, and hence $f$ does not use $\\infty $.\r\n263: This together with (\\ref{01c}) implies that $\\{f(u):u\\in V(G)\\}\\subseteq \\{0,1\\}$.\r\n264: Hence the set $S_{2}:=\\{u\\in V(G):f(u)=1\\}$ is a total dominating set of $G$ with $|S_{2}|=w(f)$.\r\n265: Thus $\\gamma _{t}(G)\\leq |S_{2}|=w(f)=\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)$.\r\n266: \r\n267: Consequently, $\\gamma ^{\\infty }(G)=\\gamma _{t}(G)$.\r\n268: \r\n269: \\item[{\\upshape(iii)}]\r\n270: For a Roman dominating function $f$ of $G$ with $w(f)=\\gamma _{R}(G)$, the function $f_{3}:V(G)\\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^{+}$ with $f_{3}(u)=\\frac{1}{2}f(u)~(u\\in V(G))$ is a $\\frac{1}{2}$-SDF of $G$ with $w(f_{3})=\\frac{1}{2}w(f)$, and hence $\\gamma ^{\\frac{1}{2}}(G)\\leq w(f_{3})=\\frac{1}{2}w(f)=\\frac{1}{2}\\gamma _{R}(G)$.\r\n271: \r\n272: Let $f'$ be a $\\gamma ^{\\frac{1}{2}}$-function of $G$.\r\n273: Then by (\\ref{01c}), $\\{f'(u):u\\in V(G)\\}\\subseteq \\{0,1,\\frac{1}{2}\\}$.\r\n274: Hence the function $f'_{3}:V(G)\\rightarrow \\{0,1,2\\}$ with $f'_{3}(u)=2f'(u)~(u\\in V(G))$ is a Roman dominating function of $G$ with $w(f'_{3})=2w(f')$.\r\n275: Thus $\\gamma _{R}(G)\\leq w(f'_{3})=2w(f')=2\\gamma ^{\\frac{1}{2}}(G)$.\r\n276: \r\n277: Consequently, $\\gamma ^{\\frac{1}{2}}(G)=\\frac{1}{2}\\gamma _{R}(G)$.\r\n278: \\qed\r\n279: \\end{enumerate}\r\n280: \r\n281: \r\n282: \r\n283: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n284: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n285: \\subsection{Main results}\\label{sec1.2}\r\n286: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n287: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n288: \r\n289: By Proposition~\\ref{prop1.1}, $c$-self-domination can continuously fill in the gaps among three invariants: domination, total domination and Roman domination.\r\n290: On the other hand, some results concerning such invariants have been proved via different techniques.\r\n291: Thus the study of $c$-self-domination for $c\\geq \\frac{1}{2}$ may give essential boundaries of them.\r\n292: As the initial research for the goal, we focus on the following known upper bounds.\r\n293: \r\n294: \\begin{Thm}[Ore~\\cite{O}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n295: \\label{ThmA}\r\n296: Let $G$ be a connected graph of order $n\\geq 2$.\r\n297: Then $\\gamma (G)\\leq \\frac{1}{2}n$.\r\n298: \\end{Thm}\r\n299: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n300: \r\n301: \\begin{Thm}[Cockayne et al.~\\cite{CDH}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n302: \\label{ThmB}\r\n303: Let $G$ be a connected graph of order $n\\geq 3$.\r\n304: Then $\\gamma _{t}(G)\\leq \\frac{2}{3}n$.\r\n305: \\end{Thm}\r\n306: %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n307: \r\n308: \\begin{Thm}[Chambers et al.~\\cite{CKPW}]%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%\r\n309: \\label{ThmC}\r\n\n"}
